**Abstract** Neutrophils, the most abundant white blood cells in the bloodstream, form the first line of defense against infections. Here, we describe a method for engineering and generating chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-neutrophils from human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs). We detail the genetic modification of hPSCs with synthetic CAR constructs and outline a chemically defined, feeder-free differentiation system that uses stage-specific signaling modulators to generate neutrophils within 3 weeks. Human neutrophils generated using this protocol express canonical neutrophil markers and exhibit characteristic neutrophil effector functions, providing a robust platform for disease modeling, neutrophil engineering, and the potential on-demand production of granulocytes for transfusion and cell-based therapies.

**Keywords:** neutrophils, human pluripotent stem cells, chemically defined differentiation, CAR-neutrophils, feeder-free culture, granulopoiesis, cell engineering, granulocyte transfusion

